WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.120
SPEAKER 1: So the best
way to experience a video

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:03.384
API is through a live demo.

00:00:03.384 --> 00:00:05.550
And let's start by taking
this video of a Super Bowl

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:06.960
commercial for Google Home.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.793
And I'm going to play
the first few seconds.

00:00:08.793 --> 00:00:11.030
We can see that it starts
with a mountain landscape.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:14.370
And we see a house,
a city street,

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.320
then it goes to a
dog, and a garage.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.879
So lots of scene changes
happening in this video.

00:00:19.879 --> 00:00:21.420
And if we were to
manually categorize

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:22.830
what was happening
throughout it,

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.330
we need to watch
the entire thing

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.940
and write down what was
happening in every scene.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.520
Luckily, the video API
takes care of this for us

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.950
with just a single
REST API request.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.180
So it tells us two things.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:36.470
One, at a high level, it tells
us what is this video about.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:39.210
And then it also tells us,
in a more granular level,

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.640
what labels it finds in
the video in each scene.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.880
So if we scroll down here, we
can see it identifies a dog,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.990
and it can tell us exactly where
in the video that dog appears.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.910
It also identifies--
at the end of the video

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.790
there's a birthday cake.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.527
And if we scroll
down a bit more,

00:00:54.527 --> 00:00:56.610
we can see that, not only
does it know it's a dog,

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.230
it knows what type of dog it is.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.682
That it's a dachshund.

00:00:59.682 --> 00:01:01.890
And if we scroll down through
the rest of the labels,

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.500
we can see that it also
successfully identifies

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.300
that mountain pass scene
from the beginning.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.475
So this is what the API
can do with one video.

00:01:09.475 --> 00:01:11.100
But you likely have
more than one video

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.340
that you want to analyze.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.340
So let's take a look at
how a company might use

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.290
the video intelligence, API.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.060
A media publisher
could have hundreds

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.081
of petabytes of video data
sitting in storage buckets.

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.830
And one common thing
they might want to do

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.840
is create a highlight
reel focused

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.970
on a specific type of content.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.164
Or search their large library
for a specific entity.

00:01:30.164 --> 00:01:32.580
So let's see how we would use
the video intelligence, API,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.490
to search a large library of
videos, given all this metadata

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.480
that we get back from it.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.337
So we've got a lot
of videos here.

00:01:39.337 --> 00:01:40.920
And let's say this
media publisher has

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.990
hours of sports
video, but they only

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.270
want to find the content
relevant to baseball.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.060
So let's go ahead and
search our library here

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.561
for baseball videos.

00:01:49.561 --> 00:01:51.060
And we can see that
not only does it

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.630
show us which videos
have baseball,

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:58.230
it tells us exactly when in
those videos baseball appears.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.120
My favorite example is this one.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.850
We have this video, which only
has a tiny bit about baseball,

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.770
but it's able to identify
that clip for us.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.591
Whereas, if we were
to manually do this,

00:02:06.591 --> 00:02:08.340
we'd have to watch the
whole video looking

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.539
for that specific scene.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:11.389
So if we click on
the scene, we can

00:02:11.389 --> 00:02:13.680
see that this is from last
year's year end search video

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.530
when the Cubs won
the World Series.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.182
So let's do one more search.

00:02:18.182 --> 00:02:20.640
I live on the East Coast, where
it's pretty cold right now.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.469
I've heard there's been a ton
of rain in SF this past year.

00:02:23.469 --> 00:02:26.010
I think we can all agree it'd
be pretty nice to be on a beach

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:27.220
right now.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.280
And while machine learning
can't take us there,

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.110
it can do the next best thing.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.800
And find all the beach clips
in our video library for us.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.230
So let's search for
our beach videos.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:41.710
And then we can click to
all of our beach clips

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.800
in the videos below.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.890
So as you saw through this demo,
the video intelligence, API,

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.560
makes it easy to quickly
and easily understand

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.570
a large library
of video content.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.370
Something that was
almost impossible

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:54.880
just a few months ago.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.000
Tasks that used
to take hours, now

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.490
take seconds with the
video intelligence, API.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.650
And I'm excited to make it
available to all of you today.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.340
Thank you.

